Spiritual Distress
Palliative & End of Life
Questioning of core beliefs, Feelings of divine abandonment, Absolute lack of internal peace

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-048
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe psychological crisis marked by profound anguish, extreme agitation, and refusal of physical care driven by existential/religious terror.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse, visiting community nurse
Urgency Justification: The community nurse requested urgent after-hours medical assessment as the patient is exhibiting severe, unmanageable psychological distress. The patient is refusing all pain medications, screaming about divine punishment, and is physically thrashing in bed. Urgent medical intervention was required to differentiate this profound spiritual terror from organic hyperactive delirium, provide immediate pharmacological anxiolysis to prevent physical injury, and establish a safe baseline prior to arranging specialized spiritual care.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I am going to hell. God has abandoned me because of what I've done, and this pain is my punishment. Do not give me those pills, I have to suffer!"
History of Present Illness: Over the last few days, the patient has exhibited profound, explicitly stated questioning of entirely previously held core beliefs and deep religious faith. Tonight, this escalated into severe, distinctly expressed feelings of complete abandonment, explicit punishment, and deep anger directly toward God. The patient verbalizes a distinct, absolute inability to find any deep meaning in the current severe suffering. There is a profoundly expressed explicit fear of death and exactly what occurs immediately after death. The patient is expressing feelings of profound, irredeemable guilt and a distinct need for deep forgiveness, linked to severely fractured relationships strictly requiring deep, urgent reconciliation before death. The patient is exhibiting a complete refusal to engage in previously comforting religious practices. The nurse notes a profound lack of internal peace, severe deep restlessness, and deep soul-level anguish. Distinct physical symptoms (tachycardia, pain exacerbation) are completely refractory to all standard medical treatments, appearing rooted strictly in deep spiritual pain.
Functional Status: Bedbound but highly agitated, constantly writhing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Refusal of essential palliative analgesia leading to a compounded physical pain crisis; profound distress of the spouse witnessing the religious terror.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 160/95 mmHg | HR: 125 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 26 bpm | SpO2: 98% on room air | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient is profoundly distressed, intensely tearful, pacing their hands over their chest, making poor eye contact, and shouting intermittently.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals the patient is fully oriented to time, person, and place, explicitly tracking conversations and demonstrating coherent, logical (though deeply anguished) thought processes. This definitively rules out organic delirium. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams reveal stress-induced tachycardia and tachypnea, but no acute cardiopulmonary failure. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the observed questioning of core beliefs, explicitly vocalized feelings of divine abandonment ("God is punishing me"), and the absolute lack of internal peace (writhing, crying).
Environmental Observations: Religious symbols (crosses, rosaries) have been actively thrown to the floor by the patient prior to arrival.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient is actively refusing scheduled oral and subcutaneous opioids, believing the physical pain is a required spiritual atonement.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Spiritual Distress - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Existential/Religious Crisis)
Differentials:

Hyperactive Terminal Delirium - Ruled out/unlikely due to the completely intact orientation, lack of visual hallucinations, and the highly specific, coherent, and philosophical nature of the distress, contrasting with the fragmented inattention of delirium.

Uncontrolled Nociceptive Pain - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary driver, because the patient is explicitly acknowledging the pain but actively choosing to refuse treatment for it based on a spiritual delusion of required punishment.

Major Depressive Disorder with Psychotic Features - Unlikely to be the sole acute diagnosis, as the distress is highly targeted toward religious abandonment rather than pervasive anhedonia, though a psychiatric overlay is present.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in the midst of a catastrophic spiritual and existential crisis. The profound psychological agony is driving massive autonomic arousal (tachycardia, hypertension) and causing a secondary physical crisis because the patient is refusing essential analgesia out of a belief in required divine punishment. Because standard reassurance and pain medications are entirely ineffective and actively rejected, delaying assessment until standard hours would subject the patient to torturous, unmanaged "total pain" and severe trauma for the spouse. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely critical to provide acute, non-judgmental validation of the spiritual terror, safely negotiate the administration of a mild anxiolytic to break the panic, and mandate urgent pastoral care involvement.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided intense, active listening, explicitly validating the reality of the patient's severe emotional pain without attempting to argue theology. Negotiated the administration of a stat sublingual dose of lorazepam, framing it strictly as a medication to "calm the racing heart" rather than to "take away the necessary pain," which the patient accepted.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the overwhelming wave of guilt and the thought that you are entirely unforgivable (trigger), explicitly ask your spouse to read the specific, pre-selected passage of comfort from your religious text out loud (behavior). Hearing the words externally interrupts the internal cycle of self-condemnation, providing a momentary, tangible anchor of grace that reduces the severe panic (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent, high-priority referral to the palliative care pastoral/spiritual care worker and the patient's own specific religious clergy for immediate morning assessment.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Multidisciplinary palliative team review tomorrow to address the ongoing refusal of analgesia.

